  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and created the alert rule. The rule is enabled and its first run came back with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has one document per web request. Its time field is `timestamp`. Each document records:\n- the client (`clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`)\n- the request (`request`, `url`, `extension`, `host`, `referer`)\n- the outcome (`response` code, `bytes` sent)\n- where it came from and went to (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`)\n- `tags`, `memory` and `hour_of_day`\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-95a004dd Web logs overview\"** has 8 panels and a default time range of the last 7 days:\n- Single numbers for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and average bytes per request\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Response code distribution (pie chart)\n- Total bytes served over time\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and total bytes\n- Top 10 source countries\n\nURL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-95a004dd-web-logs-overview-e834126db2ef\n\n**3. Rule: \"EFF-95a004dd 5xx spike\"** (id `1023428d-504b-4b5d-b81e-b94f542cfb4a`)\n- It is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using `timestamp` as the time field.\n- It looks for response codes from 500 up to (not including) 600, compared as text on `response.keyword`.\n- It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes.\n- It has no actions, so it records alerts in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone. Add a connector if you want notifications.\n\n`KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` is empty in this environment, so I couldn't call Kibana's API directly and did everything through the Kibana tools I have. That also meant I couldn't query the raw response codes. The 500\u2013600 filter assumes they are three-digit strings like \"503\", which is how the sample data stores them. I also didn't open the dashboard in a browser to check the charts.",
